tests/error_iterator_overloading_method_argument_without_default_value_after_second.glos:2:1: Error: The method 'range' is special because it implements an iterator overload

    range :: (this: Foo, that: Foo, other: bool) {}

    It should have this signature:

        range :: (this: Foo, state: &A) -> &V1, &V2, ..., bool {}

    Iteration can be by reference or by value. By default, when you implement an iterator, it only works by value.
    However you can implement both semantics using the '#reference' directive.

        range :: (this: &Iterable, state: &Iterator) -> A, #reference B, bool {}

    Notice that now, the receiver is a pointer.

        usage :: () {
            iterable: Iterable
            for a, b := range iterable {
                // Here both 'a' and 'b' are by value.
            }

            // To iterate by reference, take a reference to the iterable value.
            for a, b := range &iterable {
                // Here 'a' is by value and 'b' is by reference.
            }
        }

    It may have other optional arguments at the end, but this is the bare minimum that must be implemented.

